    "q-103191": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103191",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Given that $6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)$",
      "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6a≡6b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(6,9)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & ≡6b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ 3\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 3) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3) \\\\ 2a & ≡2b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
