    "q-103168": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103168",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$",
      "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that for $k\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe're asked to consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n3a≡3b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\text{gcd}(3,12)=3.$\n\nApplying the division property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a & ≡3b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ 3\\cdot a & ≡3\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3\\cdot 4) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot a & ≡\\cancel{3}\\cdot b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{3}\\cdot 4) \\\\ a & ≡b &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
