    "q-213286": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213286",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\n5\\cdot 2a≡5\\cdot 2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nSince $5$ and $10$ are {{field-1}}, this congruence {{field-2}} $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).$",
      "worked_solution": "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn our case, the factor $k=5$ and the modulus $n=10$ are not coprime since $\\text{gcd}(5,10)=5.$ As a result, we cannot cancel out $5$ from both sides of the congruence.\n\nTherefore, we have the following: \n\nSince $5$ and $10$ are $\\text{not coprime},$ this congruence $\\text{is not equivalent to}$ $2a≡2b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
